\par\par \b \uc2  =World Market Turmoil At A Glance: DJIA Down 34 Points \b0\par\par\uc2 398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 23:00\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- A nervous - almost panicky - pre-weekend \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market instinctively reacted to warning signs in Russia by\par buying dollars for Deutsche marks Friday. The dollar's rally\par against the German \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  translated into similar moves\par against other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , helped by safe haven-buying of\par U.S. assets, traders said. Against the yen, the dollar\par pushed back above Y146.00 and towards the Y146.48, up from\par Y144.88 a day ago\par    -- U.S. Treasury prices held their gains in late trading\par Friday, sustaining a rally that drove the yield on the new\par 30-year bond to a record low earlier in the session. In late\par trading, the benchmark 30-year bond was up 28/32 at 107\par 20/32 to yield 5.59%..\par    -- Faltering profit growth and world-wide economic\par distress suffocated an early advance in Friday's trading,\par and sent major market averages in the U.S. to more\par losses.\par    -- U.K. shares closed higher Friday, underpinned by\par gains in U.S. stocks and global equity markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  The FTSE 100\par Index of blue-chip shares climbed 55.5, or 1%, to close at\par 5455.0 points.\par    -- The thin trading volume characteristic of Friday's\par Frankfurt session petered out late afternoon save for 10\par minutes of heightened activity at the close, which pushed\par the Xetra DAX toward its earlier high of 5484.42 points. The\par key electronic index settled up 118.69 points, or 2.2%, at\par DEM5473.72\par    -- Russian stocks rebounded strongly Friday, boosted by\par heavy short-covering and hopes for another international\par bailout of Russia's finances. The Russian Trading System\par Index rose 14% to close at 115.00 points Friday, from 101.17\par points at the close of trading Thursday.\par    -- The Russian ruble (RUR) closed lower Friday amid an\par almost complete paralysis on the interbank market, as\par mistrust between banks continued to reign. The dollar was\par fixed at RUR6.3100, down from RUR6.321, thanks to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders said\par .\par    -- Russian President Boris Yeltsin said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't\par devalue the ruble.\par    -- President Clinton and Russian President Boris Yeltsin\par talked about Russia's economic crisis during a 40-minute\par telephone call this morning, but there was no discussion of\par a new aid package from the West to beleaguered Moscow,\par according to White House Spokesman Mike McCurry.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  08-14-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1700EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1141 4223 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du8e01ygo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
