\par\par \b \uc2  Russia's Prime Minister Pledges To Stand By Ruble, Austerity Plan \b0\par\par\uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 16:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MOSCOW -(Dow Jones)- The recent turmoil in Russian financial markets has not caused the government to alter its plans to defend the ruble, or shore up finances with austerity measures, Russia's prime minister told the Itar-Tass news agency Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Russian markets have been falling sharply in recent weeks on fears that the government will be forced to devalue the ruble. However, the Russian stock market appeared to steady somewhat Wednesday, with the RTS index losing about 1.6% after falling 9% on both Monday and Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Still, the ruble sank against the dollar Wednesday, after \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved to limit its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the maximum quotas of dollars it will sell to commercial banks. The banks are desperate to raise the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid this week's mass exodus from Russian financial markets. The dollar for next-day delivery closed at 6.3150 rubles, up from 6.31 at Tuesday's close. \par \par \uc2  The government has repeatedly said it will defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite the toll on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The government is also redoubling efforts to bolster budget revenues through higher tax collections and privatizations. \par \par \uc2  "No kinds of changes in the policy of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government will happen," said Prime Minister Sergei Kiriyenko. "The worse the financial situation becomes, the harder we must work to fulfill the program." \par \par \uc2  Russian Finance Minister Mikhail Zadornov echoed Kiriyenko's words, and said Wednesday the government would service Russian debt on schedule, and continue to support the ruble. \par \par \uc2  Zadornov said that redemptions in the treasury market would not be that substantial in August, thanks in part to the government's recent efforts to restructure the market and swap the obligations into dollar-denominated paper. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and state-controlled savings bank Sberbank have promised to roll over maturities and even increase holdings. \par \par \uc2  In August, redemptions on maturing government bonds total 15 billion rubles, without the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's and Sberbank's debt rollovers. \par \par \uc2  "Through the debt conversions we managed to substantially reduce the redemption payments (in August), therefore we look at the debt payments calmly," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said that tax collections continued to improve in early August, and were higher in the first 11 days of the month than in the corresponding period in July.  "Therefore we can expect that revenues in August will be bigger than in July," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said the government would double monthly tax collections to 23-25 billion rubles by the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  "If we thought that devaluation was unavoidable, we would not be taking the measures we are taking now," Zadornov said. \par \par \uc2  Zadornov confirmed that the government expects in all around $4.27 billion of loan disbursements from the International Monetary Fund in September, rejecting rumors that the fund has reduced the amount. \par \par \uc2  The expected total consists of $2.8 billion of the fund's additional loans pledged in July, a $670 million tranche of the previously-agreed loan, as well as the $800 million the fund suspended in July after Russia's Parliament rejected a number of bills aimed at increasing budget revenue. \par \par \uc2  "Naturally this (disbursement) depends on the adoption of the stabilization program," Zadornov said. \par \par \uc2  Amid the recent sharp losses on Russia's external debt, the government doesn't plan any new conversions of ruble-denominated debt into longer-term dollar borrowing. \par \par \uc2  "There isn't any demand, so it would be senseless," Zadornov said. \par \par \uc2  Amid talk that the $22.7 billion loan package already pledged to Russia by international lenders may not be enough to reassure investors, Zadornov said Russia won't use U.S. President Bill Clinton's upcoming visit to Moscow to request direct aid from Washington. \par \par \uc2  "We aren't attaching any issues of additional aid to this visit, because it wouldn't be decided during the visit," Zadornov said. "It isn't a simple procedure - and on the whole, we aren't counting on direct, bilateral aid from the G-7," the finance minister added. \par \par \uc2  Clinton is due to arrive in Moscow Sept. 1. for a three-day visit. \par \par \uc2  At his briefing Wednesday, Zadornov announced plans to streamline the Finance Ministry and tighten spending controls, a move that will involve cutting the ministry's staff by 20%. \par \par \uc2  -Paivi Munter; 7-095-974-8055; pmunter@ap.org \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du8c014xl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
