\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  Ruble Ends Lower After Panic On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Limits \b0\par\par\uc2 221 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 16:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MOSCOW (Dow Jones)--The Russian ruble sank against the dollar Wednesday, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to limit its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  caused a panic on the interbank market. \par \par \uc2  After the banks' initial rush to buy whatever amount of dollars they could, activity nearly halted, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Government bonds recovered a touch after Tuesday's massive losses. However, traders were not convinced there would be a turnaround. \par \par \uc2  The dollar for next-day delivery closed at RUR6.3100-6.3200, up from RUR6.3000-6.3200 at the Tuesday close. \par \par \uc2  Practically the whole trading session, the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained below the daily lower limit set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at RUR6.2960 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The interbank ruble market was rocked in early trading when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the maximum quotas of dollars it will sell to commercial banks. The banks are desperate to raise the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid this week's mass exodus from Russian financial markets. \par \par \uc2  "It caused a panic on the market because commercial banks count on being able to buy a certain amount (of dollars) at a fixed rate," said Dmitri Panteta, trader at International Moscow Bank. \par \par \uc2  "Those who managed, bought," he added. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 12-08-98 \par \par \