\par\par \b \uc2  Yeltsin, in Germany, hopes to win Kohl's support. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Fiona Fleck \par \uc2 721 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 1998\par 01:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BONN, June 9 (Reuters) - President Boris Yeltsin hoped to wrap up a high-profile visit to Germany on Tuesday by securing support from his closest ally in the West, Chancellor Helmut Kohl, to help stem Russia's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Kohl and Yeltsin, who met for talks over dinner late on Monday at the Bonn chancellery, discussed Russia's financial crisis "in a friendly, relaxed atmosphere", German government spokesman Otto Hauser said. \par \par \uc2  "They discussed the current state of the Russian economy and prospects for economic reforms," he said. Details of the type of help Russia might receive from its biggest trading partner Germany were still unclear. \par \par \uc2  They were due to announce the results of the two-day visit at a news conference at 0915 GMT. It was Yeltsin's first foreign appointment since the crisis was sparked two weeks ago by mass selling on Russian stock and bond markets. \par \par \uc2  Yeltsin and members of his government delegation dismissed suggestions on Monday that the Russian economy was on the verge of collapse and that they had come to beg for more loans to prop it up. \par \par \uc2  A senior economic adviser to Yeltsin said the president had no intention of asking Kohl for direct financial aid, but left open the question of whether he might seek Yeltsin's backing for further aid from the International Monetary Fund (IMF). \par \par \uc2  "We don't expect any help apart from political support," Yeltsin aide Alexander Livshits said. \par \par \uc2  Yeltsin said Moscow had no plans to devalue the rouble \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the Russian government had taken sufficient measures to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it had ample reserves to support it if necessary. \par \par \uc2  Foreign policy experts say Yeltsin is likely ask Kohl, with whom he has a warm friendship, to help him cope with the crisis. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tripled interest rates to 150 percent at the height of the financial crisis on May 27 to support the rouble, bringing them down again to 60 percent only when market pressure had eased. The rouble was steady at around 6.18 to the dollar on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Russian debt to Germany, Russia's chief Western trading partner, is 75.2 billion marks ($42.4 billion). \par \par \uc2  Although Germany has ruled out bilateral help, it is a key player in the IMF's continuing involvement in Russia. The Fund is expected to release a $670 million tranche of a $9.2 billion lending facility this month. \par \par \uc2  Yeltsin suggested in an interview with the German magazine Der Spiegel that Russia had learned from past mistakes and would not use any additional credits for "stop-gap" measures, such as paying off wage arrears. \par \par \uc2  Any future credits would be pinned to specific projects to help structural reform, Yeltsin was quoted as saying on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Kohl and Yeltsin also discussed bilateral cooperation in combating organised crime - an area of growing concern to Bonn since the collapse of Communism in the east. \par \par \uc2  It is also one of the few areas where Yeltsin can offer Kohl something in return for political backing, foreign policy experts say. \par \par \uc2  Kohl and Yeltsin also spoke about the conflict in the Serbian province of Kosovo and the recent series of nuclear tests by India and Pakistan, Hauser said. \par \par \uc2  Russian Defence Minister Igor Sergeyev, accompanying Yeltsin, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not in principle oppose NATO \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Kosovo crisis if it was conducted with the approval of the U.N. Security Council. \par \par \uc2  The possibility of NATO \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to contain the conflict has been mooted in recent days following new reports of Serb police action against Kosovo Albanian separatists. The action has prompted a flow of refugees from the region. \par \par \uc2  German Foreign Minister Klaus Kinkel and his Russian counterpart Yevgeny Primakov also met on Monday night, ahead of a big powers Contact Group meeting in London on Friday to discuss how to prevent the conflict from developing into war. \par \par \uc2  In the first agreements to concluded on the trip, Kinkel and the Russian Atomic Energy Minister Yevgeny Adamov signed two agreements on trade in nuclear materials. \par \par \uc2  The first and more controversial allows Russia to ship weapons-grade, highly enriched uranium to a research reactor in Germany. The second limits German firms' liability in the event of a nuclear accident at Russian plants they serve. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6803dyd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
