\par\par \b \uc2  Confidence in Russia still elusive - U.S. analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nailene Chou Wiest \par \uc2 497 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 1998\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 28 (Reuters) - Investors riding on the roller-coaster Russian financial market were bracing for more bumps on the downward course unless outside help came quickly, U.S. analysts and fund managers said. \par \par \uc2  While the battered ruble got a respite on Thursday - stabilising at 6.1490 per dollar - the clock was ticking as no economy could sustain Russia's 150 percent interest rates for long, they said. \par \par \uc2  The ruble's fate was now tied to the expectation for outside help, even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the flow of fund basis, was fundamentally sound, said Gretchen Rodkey, analyst at Bear Stearns and Co. \par \par \uc2  Russia is awaiting the release of a $670 billion tranche of the $9.2 billion loan from the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  "The market is expecting an additional aid package," she said. "With the expectation out, it becomes self-fulfilling." \par \par \uc2  With dwindling foreign reserves, foreign investors needed assurance that the demand for dollars would be met. "The reserves were one of the weak spots," Rodkey said. \par \par \uc2  Russia had official reserves of just over $14.5 billion, down from 15.5 billion last week after heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the ruble. \par \par \uc2  Excluding gold, however, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves amounted to only $10.5 billion, Rodkey said. \par \par \uc2  Halliwell, Chief Executive Officer of River Capital International, said Russia needed only "a few billion dollars" in addition to the IMF loan disbursement to tide it over the crisis. \par \par \uc2  The aid could come in the form of bridge loans, short of tapping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilisation fund at the IMF, he said. \par \par \uc2  A fund manager noted that the stampede to get out of the ruble assets was triggered by fears of that Russian banks might not honour the dollar forward contracts. \par \par \uc2  "The problem of Tokobank last week highlighted the counterparty risk for foreign inventors in the GKO (Russian T-bill) market," he said. \par \par \uc2  Tokobank, one of Russia's 20 largest banks, was put under "temporary administration" by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pending debt rescheduling and a new share issue. \par \par \uc2  As the yield of the three-month Russian treasury bills zoomed to 80 percent, the exceedingly high return induced some foreign investors to stay in the ruble debt market. \par \par \uc2  "The choice is between the counterparty risk and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk," he said. \par \par \uc2  But high interest rates and an emergency aid package would not get Russia out of the woods, unless they were followed immediately by other measures to rebuild investors' confidence, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors confidence had been eroding for some time as the Russian government chronically failed to bridge the fiscal gap by its attempt to raise revenues, said Keith Crane, an economist at consultancy PlanEcon Inc. \par \par \uc2  Unlike Brazil, which weathered the storm last October by raising interest rates to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Russia needed to build the basic framework to ensure a minimal level of tax collection, he said. \par \par \uc2  (( - Emerging Markets Desk, - 212-859-1674)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5s045i2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
