\par\par \b \uc2  Russia ready to offer higher yields on T-bills. \b0\par\par\uc2 251 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  VIENNA, March 14 (Reuter) - Russia plans to finance nearly half of this year's budget deficit with state securities issues and is prepared to offer higher yields to meet this target, the finance ministry's head of securities, Bella Zlatkis, said. \par \par \uc2  In a clear signal to Russia's inflation-hit financial markets that Moscow will stick to its stabilisation promises, Zlatkis said domestic debt would be a major policy tool this year to support the rouble and to soak up liquidity. \par \par \uc2  She said the government would issue state securities -- mainly short-term Treasury bills -- worth 30 trillion roubles in 1995 to help finance a 72 trillion rouble budget deficit. \par \par \uc2  "We see the domestic debt market as the most important instrument for financial stabilisation now," Zladkis told an international conference on capital markets in the former Soviet Union. \par \par \uc2  "We are also looking for ways to influence money markets to stabilise the rouble jointly with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  because \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are not enough," she said. \par \par \uc2  "We are not panicking about our domestic debt sitiuation, it must be under control. If the only solution is to issue domestic debt at high yields, we are going to do that." \par \par \uc2  Yields on Russian Treasury bills, which were first issued in May 1993, are running at annualised rates of around 300 percent. Inflation was 11 percent a month in February. \par \par \uc2  --Elif Kaban, Vienna Newsroom, 43-1-53112-258 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2